// ============================================================================
// Re-identification Scenario — Research Subject Face Leak
// ============================================================================
// The individual is a research SUBJECT. Authors attempted de-identification
// but used an ineffective method. Another researcher re-identified the face.
// ============================================================================

// === PRINCIPALS ===
activerole("research_hospital", "hospital").
activerole("lead_author", "doctor").
is_employee_of("lead_author", "research_hospital").
provider_of("lead_author", "research_subject").
activerole("academic_journal", "individual").
activerole("research_subject", "patient").
belongstorole("research_subject", "adult").
// The re-identifying researcher is at a different institution
activerole("reidentifying_researcher", "researcher").

// ============================================================================
// SCENARIO A: Research disclosure with IRB waiver, authors believed data
//             was de-identified but it wasn't actually (method was ineffective)
// ============================================================================
// Key question: Was the disclosure permitted under §512(i)?
// The authors had IRB waiver, but the "de-identified" data still contained
// an identifier (the reconstructable face).

// Sub-scenario A1: Treat as IRB-waivered research disclosure of PHI
// (recognizing that the data was NOT actually de-identified)
activerole("researcher_A1", "researcher").
has_irb_or_privacy_board_waiver("research_hospital", "researcher_A1", "research_subject", "medical-record", "research").
msg_contains("ct_scan_A1", "research_subject", "medical-record").
disclosure_attempted("research_hospital", "researcher_A1", "research_subject", "ct_scan_A1", "medical-record", "research").

// Sub-scenario A2: Treat as disclosure of data the CE BELIEVED was de-identified
// but which was actually PHI (face is reconstructable)
// If the CE genuinely believed it was de-identified, they thought they were
// disclosing DII. But since the method failed, it's actually PHI.
// We test: what does the checker say if we call it "medical-record" (PHI)?
msg_contains("ct_scan_A2", "research_subject", "medical-record").
disclosure_attempted("research_hospital", "academic_journal", "research_subject", "ct_scan_A2", "medical-record", "research").
// No IRB waiver for the journal — the hospital thought the data was de-identified
// so they didn't get a waiver for this specific disclosure

// Sub-scenario A3: What if the CE correctly characterized it as de-identified
// at the time? Test with DII attribute.
msg_contains("ct_scan_A3", "research_subject", "statistical-data").
disclosure_attempted("research_hospital", "academic_journal", "research_subject", "ct_scan_A3", "statistical-data", "research").

// ============================================================================
// SCENARIO B: Authorization-based pathway
// ============================================================================
// The research subject signed an authorization for the study.
// The authorization may have specified de-identification requirements.

// Sub-scenario B1: Valid authorization (no defect — subject consented to research use)
obtained_authorization_164_508("research_hospital", "academic_journal", "research_subject", "medical-record", "research").
// Is it defective? The subject consented to research use of their images.
// But did they consent to IDENTIFIABLE publication? If the authorization said
// "de-identified images only" and the de-identification failed, it's defective.
is_defective_authorization("auth_b1", "research_hospital", "academic_journal", "research_subject", "medical-record", "research").

msg_contains("ct_scan_B1", "research_subject", "medical-record").
disclosure_attempted("research_hospital", "academic_journal", "research_subject", "ct_scan_B1", "medical-record", "research").

// Sub-scenario B2: Authorization WITHOUT the de-identification condition
// (subject broadly consented to research use including identifiable images)
// For this test, remove the defective authorization
// This is modeled by having authorization but no defect for a different message
msg_contains("ct_scan_B2", "research_subject", "medical-record").
disclosure_attempted("research_hospital", "academic_journal", "research_subject", "ct_scan_B2", "medical-record", "research").
// The B1 authorization covers all medical-record/research disclosures from
// research_hospital to academic_journal. B1's defect also applies to B2.
// To test a non-defective version, we'd need different principal names.

// ============================================================================
// SCENARIO C: The re-identification disclosure
// ============================================================================
// The re-identifying researcher takes the "de-identified" published data
// and reconstructs the face. Is this re-identification regulated by HIPAA?
// §164.502(d)(2)(ii): "If de-identified information is re-identified,
//  a covered entity may use or disclose such re-identified information
//  only as permitted or required by this subpart."

// But: the re-identifying researcher is at a different institution.
// Is that institution a covered entity? If yes, HIPAA applies to the
// re-identified data. If no, HIPAA doesn't directly apply.

// C1: Re-identifying researcher is at a covered entity
activerole("other_university_hospital", "hospital").
is_employee_of("reidentifying_researcher", "other_university_hospital").
msg_contains("reidentified_face", "research_subject", "medical-record").
// The re-identified data is now PHI again. If the researcher's institution
// discloses it, HIPAA applies.
disclosure_attempted("other_university_hospital", "academic_journal", "research_subject", "reidentified_face", "medical-record", "research").
// No authorization, no IRB waiver for this disclosure
